{"type":"message","id":"8f49ab62","parentId":"d342636f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T12:48:15.278Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Hmm, error code 1101 is a Cloudflare error. Let me try GET first with Bearer auth to see if the auth works.\n\nWait, earlier the GET request worked fine with `X-API-Key` header and returned all the heartbeat messages. But the POST requests are returning `error code: 1101` which is Cloudflare's \"Worker exception\" or \"Script thrown exception\" error - this means the POST endpoint itself is crashing, not an auth issue.\n\nActually wait - with X-API-Key header on GET, it worked and returned data. With Bearer on GET, it returned Unauthorized. So the correct header is `X-API-Key`.\n\nFor POST, with X-API-Key it returns 1101, with Bearer it returns Unauthorized. \n\nSo it seems like the auth is working fine for POST, but the endpoint itself is throwing an exception - the 1101 error is a Cloudflare Workers error code that indicates the worker script encountered an error at runtime. The issue isn't with authentication but rather something wrong with how the request is being processed.\n\nLooking back at the GET requests, I realize only the X-API-Key header succeeded. The Bearer format seems invalid for this API. When I sent that, the server returned \"Unauthorized\" - which confirms the Bearer approach was rejected. So X-API-Key is definitely the right header for both GET and POST.\n\nFor POST with X-API-Key, I'm getting a 1101 error, which is a Cloudflare Workers internal error. This suggests the endpoint exists and auth passed, but something in the worker script itself is failing. The error is coming from the Cloudflare edge, not from an authorization failure. Let me try a different approach with the request body.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the earlier GET requests with X-API-Key worked fine and returned data successfully. But now POST requests with the same header are throwing 1101. Let me trace through what actually succeeded - it was the GET requests that worked, not the POST ones.\n\nThe 1101 error code is a Cloudflare Workers runtime error, which suggests the worker itself is crashing rather than rejecting the request. This is puzzling since the same auth and endpoint worked for GET but fails for POST. I'm wondering if there's a size limitation on the request body that's causing the worker to fail when trying to process it. The auth header for POST must be working since I'm getting a Cloudflare error rather than an \"Unauthorized\" message—if authentication failed, I'd see that instead. The 1101 error suggests the worker is encountering an internal exception, possibly related to CORS or some other runtime issue rather than an auth problem.\n\nI'm going to try simplifying the request significantly to isolate the issue, maybe reducing the payload size or checking if there's a specific request format the API expects. If that doesn't work, I'll check the cron job setup to see if messages are being delivered through a different mechanism. Let me also look at what the cron job is actually configured to do.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-32e377aa-8763-4d5d-af2a-c03048a42e96","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Let me try GET with Bearer auth to confirm auth behavior\ncurl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-a…-key\" \\\n  \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/history?threadId=arbitration&limit=1\""},"partialArgs":"{\"command\":\"# Let me try GET with Bearer auth to confirm auth behavior\\ncurl -s -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer echo-a…-key\\\" \\\\\\n  \\\"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/history?threadId=arbitration&limit=1\\\"\"}"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":117,"output":682,"cacheRead":19728,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":20527,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}}